       {
        "id": 3512446,
        "postDate": "2026-08-13T14:05:19.617Z",
        "rawMarkdown": "So good and nostalgic to see CSIRO people here 😄. @pcjimmmy @timkrige \nYes, as mentioned above, Dinov3 isn't allowed on its own, but during CSIRO the competition hosts made it explicitly clear that we can use Dinov3. Unless we get such a confirmation again during this competition, I don't believe we should be using it.\n\nAlso, the pattern seen for Dinov3 vs Dinov2 was very interesting in CSIRO. It was complicated actually. It wasn't that Dinov3 is clearly performing worse than Dinov2, or vice versa but Dinov3 actually generalized much more than Dinov2 especially when it came to private LB. \n\nI won't mention all the details but I highly recommend checking the solution writeups of CSIRO, especially for better leveraging these Dino models.  ",
        "content": "<p>So good and nostalgic to see CSIRO people here 😄. <a href=\"https://www.kaggle.com/pcjimmmy\" target=\"_blank\">@pcjimmmy</a> <a href=\"https://www.kaggle.com/timkrige\" target=\"_blank\">@timkrige</a> \nYes, as mentioned above, Dinov3 isn't allowed on its own, but during CSIRO the competition hosts made it explicitly clear that we can use Dinov3. Unless we get such a confirmation again during this competition, I don't believe we should be using it.</p>\n<p>Also, the pattern seen for Dinov3 vs Dinov2 was very interesting in CSIRO. It was complicated actually. It wasn't that Dinov3 is clearly performing worse than Dinov2, or vice versa but Dinov3 actually generalized much more than Dinov2 especially when it came to private LB. </p>\n<p>I won't mention all the details but I highly recommend checking the solution writeups of CSIRO, especially for better leveraging these Dino models.  </p>",
